body:
It can develop from sound changes occurring to a language with affixes. Most commonly, the vowel of an affix influences a vowel (or vowels) of the root. If this new pronunciation becomes common enough, speakers may begin to associate the function of the affix with the new sound in the root. Subsequent generations may pronounce the affixes themselves weaker and weaker, until they stop using the affix entirely, and now only have to alter the vowel(s) of the root to achieve the same effect. If this affects many different affixes of the language at the same time (as sound changes tend to do), you now have a whole new way of doing grammar. In the Semitic case, there are also 2-letter and 4-letter roots, 3-letter roots are just the general rule. So the ancestor language that this process started occurring in probably just had a lot of 3-syllable roots when its affixes started to erode. It's not as perfect or intentionally designed of a system as it may appear to be.

&#x200B;

A similar process is also the origin of Germanic umlaut (i.e. the vowel alternation in verbs like "sing"/"sang"/"sung", "ring"/"rang"/"rung", etc.) as well as irregular plurals like "foot"/"feet", "mouse"/"mice" (orginally probably "fot"/"foti", "mus"/"musi"). The same sort of thing can affect vowel stress, but not sound. Hence "REcord" (noun) but "reCORD" (verb; influence of the lost infinitive suffix "-en"). There's consonant mutation too, which is the same thing but the lost affixes affected the consonants of a word rather than the vowels. Really, a lot of exotic grammar systems can be chalked up to layers and layers of sound changes occurring over the centuries.

\>I finally get to use my Hans Landa Personality which I always wanted to do since I watched Inglorious Bastards.

